# Binary means binary — byte for byte, in and out.
#
# Without this, git classifies a .pdf as text on Windows and rewrites its line endings on checkout.
# Every byte offset in the PDF's xref table then points one byte early per line, and what lands in
# the working tree is a file still called .pdf that no longer opens. The commit looks clean; the
# clone is broken, and only on one platform.
#
# Caught on `fixtures/title-deed.pdf`, which exists so the document viewer can be tested against a
# real PDF rather than a text file pretending to be one.

*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# A shell script keeps LF endings wherever it is checked out.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set on the Windows machine this is developed on, and without this the
# pre-push hook is checked out with CRLF — `#!/bin/sh` followed by a carriage return is a script the
# shell refuses with "bad interpreter", so the guard silently stops guarding on exactly the platform
# it was written on. Same failure shape as the PDF above: the commit looks clean and the clone is
# broken on one platform only.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Same rule, same reason, for the container. A Dockerfile checked out with CRLF breaks line
# continuations on some daemons, and the `sh -c` entrypoint inside it is a shell script by another
# name — a stray CR at the end of a command is a character the shell tries to run.
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
compose.yml text eol=lf
